    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from early settler Thomas Owens, who built a home at the crossroads of two roads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owens_Cross_Roads%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In its early years, Owens Cross Roads depended on cotton farming, diversifying crops only in the 1920s and 1930s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owens_Cross_Roads%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A large frame business house erected near the intersection housed a post office established in 1861, a general store, and a blacksmith shop.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owens_Cross_Roads%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits in a broad valley carved by the Flint River, surrounded by Green Mountain to the west and Keel Mountain to the east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
